1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:00,384
MAN:

2
00:00:00,384 --> 00:00:03,480
How are you getting on with the assignment on handwriting?

3
00:00:03,480 --> 00:00:03,862
WOMAN:

4
00:00:03,862 --> 00:00:04,666
Not too bad.

5
00:00:04,666 --> 00:00:10,465
You know, I hadn’t realised that children benefit in so many ways from learning

6
00:00:10,465 --> 00:00:11,200
to write.

7
00:00:11,200 --> 00:00:11,250
WOMAN:

8
00:00:11,250 --> 00:00:18,393
It’s such an important skill and yet most people think handwriting is less important than in the past

9
00:00:18,393 --> 00:00:21,630
because people hardly ever write by hand these days.

10
00:00:21,630 --> 00:00:21,680
MAN:

11
00:00:21,680 --> 00:00:22,325
Yes.

12
00:00:22,325 --> 00:00:27,187
And all the evidence suggests children should learn to write by hand before they

13
00:00:27,187 --> 00:00:28,229
learn to type.

14
00:00:28,229 --> 00:00:30,660
Not least because it helps their memory.

15
00:00:30,660 --> 00:00:31,062
WOMAN:

16
00:00:31,062 --> 00:00:32,267
That’s right.

17
00:00:32,267 --> 00:00:36,284
The physical act of writing helps children to remember letters.

18
00:00:36,284 --> 00:00:39,900
That seems pretty obvious when you think about it.

19
00:00:39,900 --> 00:00:40,234
MAN:

20
00:00:40,234 --> 00:00:43,904
What’s less obvious is how it helps develop their concentration.

21
00:00:43,904 --> 00:00:47,240
They have to sit still and focus on one thing.

22
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:47,607
WOMAN:

23
00:00:47,607 --> 00:00:47,973
Yeah.

24
00:00:47,973 --> 00:00:51,640
That aspect of handwriting had never occurred to me before.

25
00:00:51,640 --> 00:00:52,024
MAN:

26
00:00:52,024 --> 00:00:52,791
Same here.

27
00:00:52,791 --> 00:00:57,780
I’m not sure I understand how it improves children’s imagination though.

28
00:00:57,780 --> 00:00:58,132
WOMAN:

29
00:00:58,132 --> 00:01:03,056
Well, there was that study which showed that primary aged children generated more ideas

30
00:01:03,056 --> 00:01:06,574
when they were writing by hand than using a keyboard.

31
00:01:06,574 --> 00:01:09,740
I would’ve guessed that would be the case.

32
00:01:09,740 --> 00:01:10,149
MAN:

33
00:01:10,149 --> 00:01:10,968
Mmm, yeah.

34
00:01:10,968 --> 00:01:14,652
I’d never associated spatial awareness with handwriting either.

35
00:01:14,652 --> 00:01:21,612
I thought spatial awareness was more to do with knowing where you are in relation to objects

36
00:01:21,612 --> 00:01:22,840
or other people.

37
00:01:22,840 --> 00:01:23,284
WOMAN:

38
00:01:23,284 --> 00:01:25,060
I thought that too.

39
00:01:25,060 --> 00:01:31,276
But good spatial awareness is essential for writing because you have to space words

40
00:01:31,276 --> 00:01:31,720
correctly.

41
00:01:31,720 --> 00:01:32,023
WOMAN:

42
00:01:32,023 --> 00:01:36,567
It’s not just fine motor skills that improve through writing as I’d always

43
00:01:36,567 --> 00:01:36,870
assumed.

44
00:01:36,870 --> 00:01:37,389
MAN:

45
00:01:37,389 --> 00:01:42,057
Handwriting is so much harder for children with dyspraxia,

46
00:01:42,057 --> 00:01:44,650
who have problems coordinating movement.

47
00:01:44,650 --> 00:01:44,856
MAN:

48
00:01:44,856 --> 00:01:47,939
It’s good there are lots of things you can do in the classroom to

49
00:01:47,939 --> 00:01:48,350
help them.

50
00:01:48,350 --> 00:01:48,698
MAN:

51
00:01:48,698 --> 00:01:53,216
They need so much more support with letter formation – you need to play

52
00:01:53,216 --> 00:01:56,344
lots of games to help them distinguish letter shapes.

53
00:01:56,344 --> 00:01:58,430
It takes a lot of patience.

54
00:01:58,430 --> 00:01:58,721
WOMAN:

55
00:01:58,721 --> 00:01:59,012
Yeah.

56
00:01:59,012 --> 00:02:03,088
I liked the idea of using one of those pens that lights up if

57
00:02:03,088 --> 00:02:04,252
you press too hard.

58
00:02:04,252 --> 00:02:06,290
That seems like a really simple solution.

59
00:02:06,290 --> 00:02:06,671
MAN:

60
00:02:06,671 --> 00:02:07,433
Yes, absolutely.

61
00:02:07,433 --> 00:02:13,529
I’m not sure there’s much you can do about children with dyspraxia writing very

62
00:02:13,529 --> 00:02:13,910
slowly.

63
00:02:13,910 --> 00:02:14,283
MAN:

64
00:02:14,283 --> 00:02:19,876
It’s more important to focus on accuracy and as they get more confident I

65
00:02:19,876 --> 00:02:21,740
think they eventually speed up.

66
00:02:21,740 --> 00:02:22,077
WOMAN:

67
00:02:22,077 --> 00:02:26,117
One quite simple thing you can do is to use grid paper.

68
00:02:26,117 --> 00:02:30,830
So they write each letter in a box and that trains them to space

69
00:02:30,830 --> 00:02:31,840
the letters correctly.

70
00:02:31,840 --> 00:02:32,145
MAN:

71
00:02:32,145 --> 00:02:32,449
Indeed,

72
00:02:32,449 --> 00:02:37,021
that’s more important for legibility than trying to get them to write in a

73
00:02:37,021 --> 00:02:37,630
straight line.

74
00:02:37,630 --> 00:02:37,991
MAN:

75
00:02:37,991 --> 00:02:43,049
For some children it might be better to teach them to write on a

76
00:02:43,049 --> 00:02:44,855
laptop rather than by hand.

77
00:02:44,855 --> 00:02:46,300
Like children with dyslexia.

78
00:02:46,300 --> 00:02:46,644
MAN:

79
00:02:46,644 --> 00:02:50,430
They often really struggle with handwriting and some just give up.

80
00:02:50,430 --> 00:02:50,777
WOMAN:

81
00:02:50,777 --> 00:02:51,124
Yeah.

82
00:02:51,124 --> 00:02:55,986
It’s not as frustrating for them if they get things wrong – on a

83
00:02:55,986 --> 00:02:59,458
keyboard they can be more willing to have a go.

84
00:02:59,458 --> 00:03:02,930
But I read that developing fluency isn’t any faster.

85
00:03:02,930 --> 00:03:03,281
MAN:

86
00:03:03,281 --> 00:03:04,335
That’s right.

87
00:03:04,335 --> 00:03:09,252
Did you read that article on the benefits of teaching print rather than cursive

88
00:03:09,252 --> 00:03:11,710
handwriting, where the letters are joined up?

89
00:03:11,710 --> 00:03:12,189
WOMAN:

90
00:03:12,189 --> 00:03:12,669
Yes.

91
00:03:12,669 --> 00:03:18,900
Well in the past cursive writing was certainly considered more stylish and educated.

92
00:03:18,900 --> 00:03:19,330
WOMAN:

93
00:03:19,330 --> 00:03:20,621
But not anymore.

94
00:03:20,621 --> 00:03:27,076
Teachers’ attitudes have changed because it’s been proved that cursive is more difficult to

95
00:03:27,076 --> 00:03:33,100
learn – especially for children with learning difficulties who find joining up letters really challenging.

96
00:03:33,100 --> 00:03:33,515
MAN:

97
00:03:33,515 --> 00:03:34,344
I agree.

98
00:03:34,344 --> 00:03:39,322
I was always worried that my poor handwriting affected my exam results.

99
00:03:39,322 --> 00:03:43,470
And now research shows that I was right to worry.

100
00:03:43,470 --> 00:03:43,747
MAN:

101
00:03:43,747 --> 00:03:48,450
I’m sure a lot of students think it’s unfair that they’re being judged on

102
00:03:48,450 --> 00:03:50,110
their handwriting not just their knowledge.

103
00:03:50,110 --> 00:03:50,467
WOMAN:

104
00:03:50,467 --> 00:03:54,394
Marks are definitely affected if examiners can’t read the script.

105
00:03:54,394 --> 00:03:59,393
That’s why it’s always been so important to teach children to write

106
00:03:59,393 --> 00:03:59,750
legibly….

107
00:03:59,750 --> 00:04:00,019
WOMAN:

108
00:04:00,019 --> 00:04:03,250
Do you think the role of handwriting will change in the future?

109
00:04:03,250 --> 00:04:03,607
MAN:

110
00:04:03,607 --> 00:04:06,108
I can’t see that changing much.

111
00:04:06,108 --> 00:04:09,324
Touch typing still isn’t taught in most schools,

112
00:04:09,324 --> 00:04:10,753
which is a shame.

113
00:04:10,753 --> 00:04:16,112
But maybe that won’t be necessary in the future because people will also be

114
00:04:16,112 --> 00:04:18,970
able to write by hand on digital devices.

115
00:04:18,970 --> 00:04:19,421
MAN:

116
00:04:19,421 --> 00:04:22,576
Anyway, teachers understand the value of handwriting.

117
00:04:22,576 --> 00:04:25,280
It’s a basic life skill.

118
00:04:25,280 --> 00:04:25,699
WOMAN:

119
00:04:25,699 --> 00:04:26,118
True.

120
00:04:26,118 --> 00:04:31,562
However, the fact is that people are writing by hand less and less

121
00:04:31,562 --> 00:04:33,656
and relying on digital devices.

122
00:04:33,656 --> 00:04:35,750
That does cause some problems.

123
00:04:35,750 --> 00:04:36,118
MAN:

124
00:04:36,118 --> 00:04:37,961
You mean like note taking?

125
00:04:37,961 --> 00:04:40,540
There are lots of apps for that.

126
00:04:40,540 --> 00:04:40,915
WOMAN:

127
00:04:40,915 --> 00:04:43,167
And for reading historical documents apparently.

128
00:04:43,167 --> 00:04:48,421
But my mum is shocked by my awful spelling and the fact that my

129
00:04:48,421 --> 00:04:49,922
punctuation is really inconsistent.

130
00:04:49,922 --> 00:04:54,050
I think you can put that down to lack of practice.

131
00:04:54,050 --> 00:04:54,507
MAN:

132
00:04:54,507 --> 00:04:55,878
I expect so.

133
00:04:55,878 --> 00:04:58,619
Personally, I miss writing by hand.

134
00:04:58,619 --> 00:05:01,360
I hardly ever write anything now.

135
00:05:01,360 --> 00:05:07,300
I remember my grandparents had such beautiful handwriting and it was so individual.

136
00:05:07,300 --> 00:05:07,621
MAN:

137
00:05:07,621 --> 00:05:11,148
Nobody I know would be able to identify my handwriting now.

138
00:05:11,148 --> 00:05:12,430
It’s a shame.

139
00:05:12,430 --> 00:05:12,810
WOMAN:

140
00:05:12,810 --> 00:05:13,571
I know.

141
00:05:13,571 --> 00:05:15,472
I feel the same way.

142
00:05:15,472 --> 00:05:20,796
I used to write a diary by hand and now I do that digitally.

143
00:05:20,796 --> 00:05:24,598
It just seems less effort to do it that way.

144
00:05:24,598 --> 00:05:27,260
So it’s not just a problem…
